WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.430
And you can see that the phosphorus has oxidized
in the air and it’s taken some of the paper

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.170
with it to generate a nice ‘P’ for phosphorus.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.210
So this is a sample of my favourite element,
this is phosphorus. Phosphorus is a very,

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:17.050
very reactive element, especially this type
of phosphorus which is white phosphorus. Phosphorus

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.760
itself, elemental phosphorus, exists as a
number of different allotropes, so a bit like

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.060
carbon with diamond and graphite, and here
we have one of these allotropes; it’s white

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:31.600
phosphorus. It exists as discrete molecules
of four phosphorus atoms connected like a

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.930
pyramid, and those four phosphorus atoms are
really strained, really reactive and they

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.340
want to really react with oxygen as quick
as they can.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:46.140
This is the sort that is used in phosphorus
bombs. It burns very easily and gives a very

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.670
intense white light out of it.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:58.579
So instantaneously you can see that fantastic
oxidation reaction as the phosphorus reacts

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:02.040
with the molecular oxygen in the gas inside
the flask.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:07.389
You can put it out quite easily by putting
water on it, provided that it hasn’t got

00:01:07.389 --> 00:01:10.719
too hot and the water will just evaporate.

00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:17.229
Very reactive form the other allotropes, black
and red phosphorus, are not as reactive and

00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:18.729
are much, much easier for us to handle.

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:24.340
Then there’s red phosphorus which is very
much less reactive which is what is used in

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:33.700
matches. It is used to make the heads of so-called
‘non-safety’ matches, which are the ones

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.799
you can strike on any sort of material, on
bricks and so on, and they tend to be this

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:40.520
rather red colour here.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.609
So the phosphorus, you’ll notice, that it’s
stored under water, and it’s stored under

00:01:44.609 --> 00:01:48.599
water to keep the oxygen and the atmosphere
away because it reacts very vigorously and

00:01:48.599 --> 00:01:54.069
can burn, hence the signs, it’s very, very
flammable. It’s so flammable in fact that

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:59.409
it burns just with the heat of the skin. So
you can see a number of scars across my hands,

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:03.549
these are all from playing with phosphorus
and doing reactions and experiments with phosphorus.

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:11.599
Phosphorus is a very important point inside
your body. Some of you may have read Aldous

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:16.530
Huxley’s book called ‘Brave New World’,
which when people died they were sent to the

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:24.390
phosphorus reclamation plant. And each average
size person contains somewhat over a pound

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:31.349
of phosphorus inside their bodies, and nowadays
people can use magnetic resonance imaging

00:02:31.349 --> 00:02:35.030
to look and see how the phosphorus is used
inside the body.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:39.640
So I’ve taken a small sample of my white
phosphorus and I’ve dissolved it in an organic

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:44.610
solvent which is carbon disulphide, because
the P4 is molecular and it dissolves really

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.349
quite nicely in this molecular solvent. So
now what we are going to do is we’re going

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:52.739
to go outside and we’re going to put this
on a piece of filter paper and then let the

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:57.980
carbon disulphide evaporate so we can see
the reaction of the phosphorus with the oxygen

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.650
and the paper.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:09.620
And the average person produces inside their
body about 70 kilos a day of a molecule called

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:16.489
ATP, adenosine triphosphate, which is the
energy producing molecule inside the body.

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:22.409
Now you don’t keep 70 kilos inside you,
or you would explode, but you’re using it

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:29.549
up all the time but in the course of the day
you synthesise nearly 70 kilos of this molecule.

00:03:29.549 --> 00:03:36.590
So as the carbon disulphide evaporates from
the paper, well there’s a couple of drips.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:40.829
And you can see that the phosphorus has oxidized
in the air and it’s taken some of the paper

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:43.640
with it to generate a nice ‘P’ for phosphorus.

